2026-06-13 13:45 PT: PM-ratified decision-record enforcement (via 360 v0.3 collaborative step, HOST+PM). Two methods in use: (1) ADRs/PDRs at docs/internal/architecture/current/adrs/ — formal, Architect-owned; (2) decisions.log (this file) — lightweight append-only log for in-session technical decisions. decisions.log was dormant since Aug 2025 (agents lost visibility during Code migration). Fix: add to CLAUDE.md Quick Reference + route to role briefings so agents know both surfaces exist and when to use each. HOST routing to Arch+Docs.

2026-06-14 16:11 PT: PM-ratified connector topology: MCP, not native. Piper consumes GitHub/Slack/Calendar/Notion via MCP servers (Piper as MCP *consumer*), retiring the bespoke per-connector clients. PM rationale: "the direction everyone is moving in; native is dated and clunky." Decision made 2026-06-14 AM; recorded here post-hoc during the RECONNECT audit cascade (it had lived only in scope doc §0 + an Arch memo + session logs, not in this log). ADR (MCP-consumer substrate, auth model, per-connector migration path, MCP-server maturity per connector) is Arch-owned and in progress = #1232 / RECONNECT-WS5. Audit note: ADR-001 (MCP consumer pilot) supports; ADR-052 (tool-based, no separate servers) needs reconciliation; ADR-058 (multi-tenancy) governs the auth layer.

2026-06-15 08:55 PT: ADR-070 filed v0.1 (Architect) — MCP-Consumer Connector Architecture. Resolves the architectural-how following PM's 2026-06-14 direction-ratification. Key decisions: (1) Piper as MCP consumer; one Connector protocol (`connect`/`status`/`resolve`/`degrade`) all MCP-consumer adapters implement; (2) ADR-052 reconciled — applies to Piper-as-host (tool surface), ADR-070 to Piper-as-consumer (downstream integrations); no actual tension; (3) MCP server owns OAuth/token lifecycle; Piper stores per-user MCP-server bindings only (not raw creds); (4) DB-backed user-scoped config kills `data/*_preferences.json` cwd-relative flat files; (5) maturity-tiered migration with Slack/Notion escape valve if MCP servers immature; (6) WS-9 identity unification is prerequisite to WS-1, not parallel; (7) frames much of RECONNECT as finishing ADR-058 implementation, not greenfield. Unblocks Lead Dev decomposition. M4/M5 placement is PPM's call.

2026-06-15 09:0x PT: ADR-071 v0.1 filed (Lead-authored, Arch-ratify pending) — User-Auth Anchoring Pattern for Content Stores, from the #1241 audit (PM-directed systemic flag). Key decisions: (D1) anchoring required when content is user-attributable AND per-user-rendered; PM-domain cluster (products/features/work_items/intents/workflows/tasks) is global-by-design with three non-negotiable disciplines (explicit is_global_pm_domain exemption + per-user-render guard at the consumer boundary + tenant_id multi-tenant migration path) — so Radar's #1239 WorkItem leg needs the render-guard, NOT a schema change. (D2) canonical convention = owner_id UUID FK→users.id; user_id string DEPRECATED; no-anchor FORBIDDEN — the PM-endorsed consolidating refactor, m-40 shim migration. (D3) scoping-filtered-at-read invariant (the (a,3) leak paths: conversations.get_by_id, insights.get_for_object, knowledge_nodes conditional, artifacts post-hoc). (D4, half the ADR weight) principal threaded as a required param from the host boundary; the `intent.context.get("user_id") if … else None` opportunistic re-fetch (40+ sites) is the named anti-pattern. (D5) AST guards (m-41). (D6) layer-then-migrate, doc store + stakeholders first (privacy (c,3) gaps). (D7) names but does not pre-author the multi-tenant path. Companion to ADR-058 at content altitude. Lead loops Arch for ratification.
